      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a large pot over medium heat. Fry onion for 5 minutes. Add garlic, ginger, turmeric, cumin and cinnamon. Cook 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato puree and canned tomatoes. Stir and cook for 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add red lentils, chickpeas, coriander stems, parsley and 1.2 litres water. Bring to a boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce heat and simmer for 30 minutes until lentils are completely soft."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Whisk flour and water to a smooth paste. Stir into the soup over medium heat. Cook for 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until the soup thickens."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add lemon juice and coriander leaves. Taste and adjust salt. Serve with flatbread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Soup too thick",
              "cause": "Flour paste over-thickened or too much lentil",
              "fix": "Stir in 200ml hot water and bring back to a simmer. Harira should pour easily but coat a spoon."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Flour paste lumpy",
              "cause": "Added to too-hot liquid or not mixed smoothly first",
              "fix": "Always mix the flour and cold water separately until completely smooth before adding to the pot. Stir constantly as it cooks."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Harira is served every evening during Ramadan across Morocco, eaten with dates, chebakia (honey pastries) and hard-boiled eggs to break the fast. It is sold at street stalls around mosques at the call to Maghrib prayer. Outside Ramadan it appears as a starter in Moroccan restaurants and as a weekday lunch staple. There are dozens of regional versions: some include lamb, some vermicelli, others are entirely plant-based."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
